{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores in silicon-carbon anodes as stress-regulation units to mitigate lithiation-induced deformation and interphase degradation. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the material redirects expansion stress internally, reducing electrode swelling. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity, 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, with 13.1% thickness expansion after 50 cycles. The design enables scalable production and high cycle stability in lithium-ion batteries.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores in heteroatom-doped carbon hosts redirect lithiation-induced stress internally, suppressing electrode swelling",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composites achieve 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C",
    "The design reduces thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles and enables 80% capacity retention in 1200 cycles"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore engineering as a scalable strategy for developing mechanically stable silicon-carbon anodes with minimal expansion and enhanced cycle life in lithium-ion batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study integrated single-cell epigenomic data from 2.5 million adult mouse brain nuclei, profiling four histone modifications, transcriptomes, chromatin accessibility, DNA methylation, and 3D genome organization. The resulting atlas identified 100+ cell subclasses and defined active, primed, and repressive chromatin states across 81% of the genome. Active states with combinatorial histone marks better predict functional enhancers than accessibility alone, while H3K27me3 and H3K9me3 states indicate distinct repression mechanisms. This resource enables deep learning models for predicting cell-type-specific epigenomic features and gene expression from DNA sequence.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The atlas spans over 100 brain cell subclasses using integrated epigenomic profiling of 2.5 million nuclei.",
    "Active chromatin states defined by combinatorial histone modifications better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone.",
    "H3K27me3- and H3K9me3-marked chromatin states delineate distinct modes of cell-type-specific gene repression."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This multimodal epigenomic atlas provides a comprehensive framework for annotating the brain regulatory genome and interpreting non-coding disease risk variants through cell-type-specific epigenomic features."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing the biodegradation of compostable polyesters in deep-sea environments by embedding enzymes within them. The researchers thermally incorporated enzymes into PBS, PBSA, PCL, and PBAT polyesters, demonstrating accelerated degradation at depths up to 5,503 meters. Enzyme-embedded polyesters achieved near-complete degradation at 757 meters within five months and showed moderate biodegradation at extreme depths. The approach maintains mechanical properties while enabling efficient mineralization of plastic waste in marine settings.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Enzyme-embedded polyesters degraded near-completely at 757 m deep sea within 5 months",
    "Degradation occurred at extreme depths (5,503 m) with moderate biodegradation rates",
    "Mechanical properties of enzyme-embedded polyesters matched enzyme-free counterparts"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This strategy provides a scalable method for accelerating marine plastic degradation, addressing a critical gap in deep-sea compostable plastic breakdown."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how protein kinase A (PKA) signaling regulates group 3 innate lymphoid cell (ILC3) homeostasis and IL-23-driven gut inflammation. Using a single-cell atlas, researchers found that prostaglandin activates PKA to promote tissue-protective functions, while IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors like PRKAR1A. Deletion of Prkar1a reduced ILC3s and increased infection susceptibility in mice, and human ILC3s express PRKAR1A, which is downregulated by IL-23 in inflammation. These results indicate PKA signaling is critical for ILC3 tissue protection, with IL-23 causing pathological PKA activation in gut inflammation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IL-23 suppresses PKA inhibitors (PRKAR1A) in ILC3s",
    "Prkar1a deletion reduced ILC3s and increased enteric infection susceptibility in mice",
    "Human intestinal ILC3s express PRKAR1A, downregulated by IL-23 in inflammatory bowel disease"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes PKA signaling as a key regulator of ILC3 homeostasis, revealing how IL-23 disrupts this balance to drive gut inflammation through targeted molecular mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how two distinct proteins, which permeabilize mitochondria, trigger cell death via separate pathways. The research identified that each protein operates through unique mechanisms to induce apoptosis, with no overlap in their molecular actions. The findings suggest that mitochondrial permeabilization can be regulated by multiple independent pathways, potentially offering insights into targeted cancer therapies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Two proteins independently permeabilize mitochondria to initiate cell death",
    "Each protein utilizes distinct molecular mechanisms without overlapping pathways",
    "The dual pathways highlight potential targets for modulating apoptosis in disease contexts"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that mitochondrial permeabilization can be mediated by multiple non-redundant pathways, advancing understanding of cell death regulation and its therapeutic implications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates kinetic-environmental coupling at the buried interface in ambient flexible perovskite solar cells. Using dual-side in situ photoluminescence, it reveals simultaneous nucleation at gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during antisolvent processing. Outward solvent evaporation delays α-phase conversion at the buried interface, exacerbating moisture attack. The researchers designed a self-segregating molecule (NFA) that enriches at the buried interface to synchronize crystallization, passivate defects, and form a hydrophobic barrier. This approach achieved 24.84% power conversion efficiency in small cells and 21.46% in mini-modules, maintaining 96.26% efficiency after 1000h damp aging at 85±5% RH.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Simultaneous nucleation occurs at both gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during antisolvent processing",
    "Outward solvent evaporation delays α-phase conversion at the buried interface, worsening moisture ingress",
    "Self-segregating molecule NFA synchronizes crystallization, passivates defects, and forms a hydrophobic barrier"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes self-segregated interfacial regulation as a rational strategy to overcome kinetic-environmental constraints in ambient flexible perovskite solar cells."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a dual-copper catalytic system enabling switchable chlorine atom transfer radical reactions (ATRA/ATRP). The approach uses a dinuclear copper complex to activate unactivated alkyl chlorides' strong C-Cl bonds while a mononuclear copper catalyst controls C-Cl bond reconstruction. The reaction switches between ATRA and ATRP modes based on mononuclear copper presence, facilitating synthesis of diverse small molecules and α-end-functionalized polyacrylates, and direct PVC modification under mild conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A dinuclear copper complex selectively activates unactivated alkyl chlorides' C-Cl bonds",
    "Mononuclear copper catalyst mediates controllable C-Cl bond reconstruction",
    "Switchable reaction pathway enables ATRA/ATRP-type reactions with distinct applications"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a catalytic strategy for versatile chlorine atom transfer reactions using unactivated alkyl chlorides, advancing synthetic methods for functionalized small molecules and polymers."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether an electronic device could regulate glucose metabolism in mice by controlling redox mechanisms. The researchers developed a device that modulates redox processes to influence glucose concentrations. Results showed the device successfully maintained target glucose levels in mice, demonstrating potential for electronic glucose regulation without pharmacological intervention.",
  "key_findings": [
    "An electronic device modulates red:ox mechanisms to regulate glucose concentrations in mice",
    "The device maintains target glucose levels without pharmacological intervention",
    "This approach provides a non-pharmacological method for glucose control"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a novel electronic approach for glucose regulation, offering a potential alternative to pharmacological methods for metabolic control."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates amorphization of organic small molecule 2,3,7,8-tetraamino-5,10-dihydrophenazine-1,4,6,9-tetraone (TDT) to enhance active site exposure in aqueous ammonium-ion batteries. By disrupting π-π stacking, amorphous TDT achieves high surface area (738 m² g⁻¹), delivers 242.8 mAh g⁻¹ at 0.1 A g⁻¹, and maintains 87.1% capacity after 817 cycles. In situ measurements confirm NH₄⁺ adsorption via coordination/deprotonation and hydrolysis-driven proton storage. The TDT||CuFe-PBA cell retains 100% capacity over 100,000 cycles at 5 A g⁻¹, demonstrating practical Ah-level AAIB performance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Amorphous TDT disrupts π-π stacking to expose buried redox sites",
    "Amorphous TDT achieves 242.8 mAh g⁻¹ capacity at 0.1 A g⁻¹ with 87.1% retention after 817 cycles",
    "TDT||CuFe-PBA full cell maintains 100% capacity over 100,000 cycles at 5 A g⁻¹"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "Amorphization of organic small molecules enables high-capacity, durable aqueous ammonium-ion batteries by activating previously inaccessible redox sites through structural disruption."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a single-molecule spin-photon interface using a triplet ground-state carbene embedded in a host crystal. The approach demonstrates narrow zero-phonon lines, spectral stability exceeding one hour, spin-selective transitions, and single-molecule optically detected magnetic resonance. Results include millisecond-scale coherence and tens-of-milliseconds spin relaxation at 4.5 K, establishing molecular qubits as viable for quantum optics applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Narrow zero-phonon lines with high spectral stability over one hour",
    "Spin-selective optical transitions and single-molecule optically detected magnetic: magnetic resonance",
    "Millisecond-scale dynamical-decoupling coherence and tens-of-milliseconds spin relaxation at 4.5 K"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a molecular platform capable of robust spin-photon interfaces, advancing quantum networking by providing a bottom-up designed system with long spin lifetimes and optical coherence."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether avian cognition could surpass primates in decision speed. Using carrion crows as subjects, researchers compared decision-making efficiency against humans and monkeys. Results showed carrion crows made decisions significantly faster than both humans and monkeys, suggesting avian brains may possess unique cognitive processing capabilities for rapid decision-making.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Carrion crows outperformed humans and monkeys in decision speed",
    "Avian decision-making efficiency exceeds primate counterparts",
    "Carrion c: 100% faster than humans and monkeys in decision tasks"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This finding demonstrates that avian species can achieve superior cognitive processing speed in specific decision-making contexts compared to primates, expanding understanding of cognitive diversity across vertebrate species."
}

{
  "summary": "This review establishes a state-centered framework for organic mixed ionic-electronic conductors (OMIECs), moving beyond scalar descriptors to model electrochemical operation as multidimensional, history-dependent state trajectories. It introduces minimal sufficient state bases to distinguish essential coordinates, while waveform history and spatial heterogeneity determine when lumped descriptions remain valid. The framework enables experimental testing of relationships like congruence and kinetic ordering, and recasts electrochromics, OECTs, and energy devices as trajectory-selecting technologies within a shared electrochemical-state landscape.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A state-centered framework replaces scalar descriptors with multidimensional, history-dependent state trajectories for OMIECs",
    "Minimal sufficient state bases identify essential coordinates, distinguishing them from redundant ones",
    "Waveform history and spatial heterogeneity determine validity of lumped descriptions in electrochemical systems"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This framework provides a unified approach to model and design electrochemical trajectories in organic mixed conductors, bridging molecular structure, device architecture, and operational protocols for enhanced functionality and durability."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the synthesis and electronic properties of atomically precise carbon pentaheptite nanoribbons (CPHNs) via on-surface assembly. Researchers created two narrow CPHN types with distinct electronic behaviors: CPHN-1 functions as an intrinsic metal with near-ballistic conductance near one conductance quantum, while CPHN-2 acts as a narrow-bandgap semiconductor. The findings demonstrate potential for all-carbon electronics and quantum-limited conductors.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Two narrow carbon pentaheptite nanoribbons (CPHNs) were synthesized with distinct electronic properties",
    "CPHN-1 exhibits intrinsic metallic behavior with near-ballistic conductance approaching one conductance quantum",
    "CPHN-2 functions as a narrow-bandgap semiconductor"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The realization of atomically precise metallic carbon pentaheptite nanoribbons provides a pathway to quantum-limited all-carbon conductors for next-generation electronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the emergence of a Chern metal in frustrated moiré Kondo lattices. Using angle-aligned MoTe2/WSe2 bilayers, researchers observed a compressible metallic state with nearly quantized Hall resistance and finite longitudinal resistance. Nonlocal transport and magnetic circular dichroism confirmed chiral edge states and a magnetization plateau before Kondo breakdown. The findings demonstrate how moiré engineering can stabilize topological quantum phases through band topology and Kondo interactions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Observation of a compressible Chern metal at the onset of magnetic Kondo breakdown in MoTe2/WSe2 bilayers",
    "Nearly quantized Hall resistance and finite longitudinal resistance indicating a bad metallic bulk",
    "Magnetization plateau preceding Kondo breakdown, confirmed by nonlocal transport and magnetic circular dichroism"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes moiré engineering as a pathway to stabilize topological quantum phases by interplaying band topology and Kondo interactions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates shear alignment polymer extrusion (SHAPE) 3D printing for fabricating high-loading nanocomposites with long-range ordered nanostructures. The approach uses high shear forces to align nanofillers like boron nitride nanosheets and carbon nanotubes within polymer matrices. Results demonstrate scalable production of 3D-printed composites with 55 wt% boron nitride in polylactic acid, achieving zero volume shrinkage and exceptional thermomechanical stability. Vertically aligned structures show enhanced thermal conductivity (1.07 W/m/K), while horizontally aligned ones provide thermal insulation (0.071 W/m/K).",
  "key_findings": [
    "SHAPE enables scalable 3D printing of nanocomposites with up to 55 wt% boron nitride nanosheets",
    "Printed composites exhibit zero volume shrinkage and stability beyond polymer melting temperatures",
    "Vertically aligned BN composites achieve thermal conductivity of 1.07 W/m/K, while horizontally aligned structures offer thermal insulation of 0.071 W/m/K"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This method establishes a pathway for cross-scale design of functional nanocomposites with tailored thermal and mechanical properties through precise nanofiller alignment during additive manufacturing."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines molecular mechanisms driving inflammatory rheumatic diseases including rheumatoid arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and systemic lupus erythematosus. It analyzes dysregulation of innate and adaptive immune responses and evaluates emerging therapeutic targets that enable improved disease control and potential cures for previously chronic conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dysregulation of innate and adaptive immune responses underlies inflammatory rheumatic diseases",
    "Emerging therapeutic targets are enabling more robust disease control",
    "First prospects for curing previously chronic inflammatory rheumatic diseases exist"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This review advances understanding of molecular pathways in rheumatic diseases and identifies promising therapeutic strategies that could transform chronic inflammatory conditions from progressive disorders to curable states."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies a Martian meteorite (Teghaza 001) formed ≳4.1 billion years ago as an ancient crustal sample. The research characterizes its composition, mineralogy, and texture, revealing it originated from a geochemically distinct mantle source with primordial composition. The meteorite shows aqueous alteration with 18O-enriched fluids, indicating early Mars had liquid water interacting with crustal rocks.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Teghaza 001 is a 4.1-billion-year-old Martian crustal sample",
    "It formed from a geochemically distinct mantle source with primordial composition",
    "Aqueous alteration with 18O-enriched fluids occurred before ejection"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This discovery provides direct evidence of Mars' early crustal formation and fluid-rock interactions, advancing understanding of the planet's ancient geological history and potential for early habitable conditions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the long-standing challenge of achieving stable photocatalytic pure water splitting over Ta3N5 by identifying incompatibility in cocatalysts between alkaline water oxidation systems and pure water splitting as a critical bottleneck. The researchers developed a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst using a multi-step incipient wetness impregnation strategy. The optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx photocatalyst achieved stoichiometric pure water splitting with an apparent quantum yield of 0.72% at 420 nm, the highest reported for Ta-based nitrides/oxynitrides. Mechanistic insights indicate enhanced interfacial electric fields and reduced reaction barriers improve hole extraction and surface kinetics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of cocatalyst incompatibility between alkaline water oxidation and pure water splitting as a bottleneck for Ta3N5 photocatalysis",
    "Development of RuCoOx cocatalyst via multi-step incipient wetness impregnation",
    "Optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx achieved 0.72% apparent quantum yield for stoichiometric pure water splitting at 420 nm"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cocatalyst compatibility as a fundamental design principle for stable photocatalytic water splitting in nitride/oxynitride materials, providing mechanistic insights for advancing solar-driven water splitting technologies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates cardiac metabolism by identifying MCT1-dependent lactate import as a critical pathway for sustaining energy production when mitochondrial pyruvate carrier (MPC) is absent. Using genetic and pharmacological approaches, it demonstrates that MCT1 enables mitochondrial lactate conversion to py. In MPC-deficient hearts, MCT1 loss impairs lactate oxidation, reduces metabolic flexibility, and accelerates heart failure under stress.",
  "key_findings": [
    "MCT1 is required for glucose carbon oxidation in MPC-deficient myocardium",
    "MCT1 localizes to the inner mitochondrial membrane in cardiomyocytes",
    "MCT1 loss impairs lactate oxidation and accelerates heart failure under stress"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a parallel lactate-based metabolic pathway that sustains cardiac energy production during metabolic stress, providing a mechanism for myocardial resilience against injury and stress."
}

{
  "summary": "This study examines the impact of AI on mathematical discovery, focusing on OpenAI's solution to the Navier-Stokes problem and subsequent publications. The research assesses whether AI-driven mathematical progress can replicate the human thrill of discovery. Findings indicate AI has accelerated problem-solving but may diminish the intrinsic satisfaction of understanding complex concepts through human-driven insight.",
  "key_findings": [
    "OpenAI resolved the Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness problem using AI agents",
    "AI systems generated 722 manuscripts addressing 372 mathematical problem families",
    "AI-driven solutions risk reducing the emotional satisfaction of mathematical discovery"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study highlights AI's capacity to rapidly advance mathematical proofs while raising questions about the irreplaceable human element in the discovery process."
}

{
  "summary": "This study quantifies magnetic interactions in Fe3GeTe2/graphite/Fe3GeTe2 heterostructures using cross-sectional Lorentz transmission electron microscopy and off-axis electron holography. It reconstructs local projected magnetic induction within and between layers, revealing domain alignment weakens with increasing separation. The stray-field coupling length scale is λ=37±7 nm, corresponding to a 23% reduction in interlayer magnetic induction relative to bulk FGT. This near-surface effect guides device design requiring controlled magnetic coupling.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Stray-field coupling length scale of λ=37±7 nm for FGT/graphite/FGT heterostructures",
    "23% reduction in interlayer magnetic induction relative to bulk FGT at λ",
    "Near-surface magnetic induction reduction up to ∼100 nm from a surface"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides quantitative metrics for magnetic coupling in stacked van der Waals magnets, enabling precise engineering of magnetic interactions in heterostructure-based devices."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates why early humans settled only briefly in specific desert regions despite abundant water. Using a 1,500-km geoarchaeological survey across Arabia's Rub' al Khali desert, researchers found that the availability of stone for tool production was a critical factor alongside water. Palaeolake deposits existed at 55 of 125 sites, but no lithic artifacts or knappable materials were present where water was accessible. The analysis indicates early human settlement required both water and lithic resources, challenging models that prioritize water availability alone.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Palaeolake deposits were present at 55 of 125 surveyed sites in Rub' al Khali, yet no lithic artifacts or knappable materials were found where water was accessible.",
    "Spatial modeling shows 57% of water-accessible terrain lacks knappable stone, with raw-material availability improving site discrimination from an area under the curve of 0.695 to 0.768.",
    "Convergent patterns across Africa, North America, and Asia suggest stone availability, not water alone, constrained early human dispersal into deserts."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate that lithic resource availability, not water, was a key constraint on early human settlement in arid regions, refining dispersal models by integrating geological factors."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether Western Pacific double subduction drives rapid rifting in the Gulf of California. Using 3D geodynamic models, it demonstrates that subduction reorganization transmits extensional stresses of 5–15 MPa across the Pacific plate, loading the Gulf of California oblique rift (GOCOR) and southern San Andreas fault. The findings suggest double subduction reorganization may initiate rapid rifting in a preconditioned lithosphere.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Subduction reorganization transmits extensional stresses of 5–15 MPa across the Pacific plate",
    "This loading mechanism explains rapid rifting in the Gulf of California oblique rift (GOCOR)",
    "Double subduction reorganization may initiate rapid rifting in a preconditioned lithosphere"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study identifies double subduction reorganization as a key driver for rapid continental rifting, providing a mechanism for how external tectonic forces can initiate rifting in preconditioned lithospheres."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines aluminum scandium nitride (AlScN) as a next-generation semiconductor for ferroelectric and piezoelectric applications. It investigates physical mechanisms behind enhanced ferroelectric switching and piezoelectric responses via scandium doping in aluminum nitride. The study identifies challenges in high-quality epitaxial film growth, including ferroelectric endurance limits, interface instability, and leakage currents. Recent advances in AlScN applications for ferroelectric memories, radio-frequency circuits, and high-electron-mobility transistors are highlighted, emphasizing potential for energy-efficient computing and AI hardware.",
  "key_findings": [
    "AlScN exhibits improved ferroelectric switching and piezoelectric responses compared to pure AlN due to scandium doping",
    "High-quality AlScN epitaxial films face challenges such as reduced ferroelectric endurance and interface instability",
    "Recent progress enables AlScN applications in ferroelectric memories, radio-frequency circuits, and high-electron-mobility transistors"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This review advances understanding of AlScN's role in enabling energy-efficient computing and AI hardware through optimized ferroelectric and piezoelectric mechanisms."
}
